{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KdYu8bXzQcWH4psQE7aXcU","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have everything I need. Let me create a comprehensive summary of the multi-stream pattern used in this codebase.\n\n## Summary of Multi-Stream Pattern in CUDA Rust Codebase\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the codebase, here are the key findings about the multi-stream pattern:\n\n### 1. **NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS and Threading Constants**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:99`\n\n```rust\nconst NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS: usize = 8;\n```\n\nThis constant defines the maximum number of OS threads (and thus CUDA streams) for parallel Round 0 processing. The comments note: \"More threads = more concurrent kernels but more peak GPU memory and more MemoryManager mutex contention. 8 provides good SM utilization on the RTX 4090.\"\n\n### 2. **Multi-Threading Pattern with `std::thread::scope`**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1153-1176`\n\nThe pattern uses Rust's `std::thread::scope` for managed multi-threading with automatic cleanup:\n\n```rust\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n        .into_iter()\n        .zip(thread_items.into_iter())\n        .enumerate()\n        .map(|(thread_id, (mut bufs, indices))| {\n            let items = &work_items;\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<Vec<Round0AirResult>, LogupZerocheckError> {\n                let t0 = std::time::Instant::now();\n                let results: Vec<_> = indices\n                    .iter()\n                    .map(|&idx| process_air_round0(&items[idx], &mut bufs))\n                    .collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;\n                tracing::debug!(thread_id, elapsed_ms = t0.elapsed().as_millis(), num_airs = indices.len(), \"round0 thread done\");\n                Ok(results)\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n    let mut all_results = Vec::with_capacity(work_items.len());\n    for handle in handles {\n        all_results.extend(handle.join().unwrap()?);\n    }\n    Ok::<_, LogupZerocheckError>(all_results)\n})?\n```\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- Each thread spawned handles multiple AIRs sequentially from its assigned work items\n- Round-robin distribution: thread i gets items i, i+N, i+2N, etc. for balanced load (items sorted by descending height)\n- Results collected and scattered back into the main batch polynomial\n\n### 3. **Per-Thread GPU Buffers**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs:26-33`\n\n```rust\npub(crate) struct Round0ThreadBuffers {\n    pub zc_intermediates: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n    pub zc_temp_sums: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    pub logup_intermediates: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n    pub logup_temp_sums: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n}\n```\n\nPre-allocated buffers per thread to avoid per-AIR mutex contention on the global memory manager. These are reused across all AIRs processed by that thread.\n\n### 4. **Per-Thread Buffer Pre-Allocation Strategy**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1086-1131`\n\nThe pattern uses 95th percentile sizing to balance memory usage:\n\n```rust\n// Pre-compute buffer sizes for pre-allocation using a percentile threshold.\n// Using the max would require enormous buffers (driven by a few large AIRs),\n// causing GPU memory pressure that degrades all phases. The 95th percentile\n// covers 95%+ of AIRs while keeping total allocation manageable.\nlet percentile_idx = |n: usize| -> usize { (n * 95 / 100).min(n.saturating_sub(1)) };\nlet (max_zc_intermed, max_zc_temp_sums, max_logup_intermed, max_logup_temp_sums) = {\n    // ... collect sizes and sort\n};\n\n// Pre-allocate per thread\n(0..num_threads)\n    .map(|_| round0::Round0ThreadBuffers {\n        zc_intermediates: if max_zc_intermed > 0 {\n            DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_zc_intermed)\n        } else {\n            DeviceBuffer::new()\n        },\n        // ... similar for other buffers\n    })\n    .collect()\n```\n\nMemory budget check reduces thread count if necessary:\n```rust\nwhile num_threads > 1 && num_threads * per_thread_bytes > 2_000_000_000 {\n    num_threads /= 2;\n}\n```\n\n### 5. **current_stream_sync() Function**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs:81-83`\n\n```rust\npub fn current_stream_sync() -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    check(unsafe { cudaStreamSynchronize(cudaStreamPerThread) })\n}\n```\n\nSynchronizes the per-thread CUDA stream. This is imported in the logup_zerocheck module and used to ensure GPU work is visible before CPU-side async frees happen (for peak memory tracking).\n\n### 6. **cudaStreamPerThread Constant**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs:71`\n\n```rust\npub const cudaStreamPerThread: cudaStream_t = 0x02 as cudaStream_t;\n```\n\nMagic constant (0x02) that tells CUDA to use the thread's per-thread default stream, enabling automatic work serialization within each OS thread without explicit stream management.\n\n### 7. **FFI Function Signatures**\n\n#### `_stacked_reduction_r0_required_temp_buffer_size`\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs:37-41`\n\n```rust\npub fn _stacked_reduction_r0_required_temp_buffer_size(\n    trace_height: u32,\n    trace_width: u32,\n    l_skip: u32,\n) -> u32;\n```\n\nComputes the required temporary buffer size for the block_sums intermediate results (in FpExt elements).\n\n#### `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0`\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs:44-54`\n\n```rust\nfn _stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0(\n    eq_r_ns: *const EF,\n    trace_ptr: *const F,\n    lambda_pows: *const EF,\n    block_sums: *mut EF,\n    output: *mut EF,\n    trace_height: u32,\n    trace_width: u32,\n    l_skip: u32,\n    num_x: u32,\n) -> i32;\n```\n\nComputes three partial sums (G0, G1, G2) per z-coordinate. Uses two-stage reduction: block-level to `block_sums`, then final reduction that **ADDs** to `output` for bucket-based accumulation.\n\n#### `_stacked_reduction_fold_ple`\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs:56-64`\n\n```rust\nfn _stacked_reduction_fold_ple(\n    src: *const F,\n    dst: *mut EF,\n    omega_skip_pows: *const F,\n    inv_lagrange_denoms: *const EF,\n    trace_height: u32,\n    trace_width: u32,\n    l_skip: u32,\n) -> i32;\n```\n\nParallelizes barycentric interpolation across `2^l_skip` threads per output cell. Each output is computed independently with no need for synchronization across cells.\n\n### 8. **CUDA Kernel ADD Pattern**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sumcheck.cuh`\n\nThe final reduction kernel uses a template-parameterized ADD_TO_OUTPUT pattern:\n\n```cpp\nif (tid == 0) {\n    if constexpr (ADD_TO_OUTPUT) {\n        output[out_idx] += sum;  // ADD to existing value\n    } else {\n        output[out_idx] = sum;   // WRITE new value\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` calls this with `ADD_TO_OUTPUT=true`:\n\n```cpp\nsumcheck::final_reduce_block_sums<true>\n    <<<output_size, reduce_block, reduce_shmem>>>(block_sums, output, num_blocks);\n```\n\n### 9. **Elementwise Addition Kernel**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu:815-820`\n\n```cpp\n__global__ void add_alpha_kernel(FracExt *data, size_t len, FpExt alpha) {\n    size_t idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (idx < len) {\n        data[idx].q = data[idx].q + alpha;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis provides a general-purpose elementwise addition pattern that could be reused for per-thread buffer reduction. Exposed via FFI:\n\n```cpp\nextern \"C\" int _frac_add_alpha(FracExt *data, size_t len, FpExt alpha) {\n    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(len);\n    add_alpha_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(data, len, alpha);\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### 10. **Threading Decision Logic**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1062-1070`\n\n```rust\nlet mut num_threads = if work_items.len() >= 100 {\n    NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS.min(work_items.len())\n} else {\n    1\n};\n```\n\nMulti-threading only activates with 100+ AIRs to avoid overhead with few large AIRs where each kernel already saturates the GPU.\n\n### **Key Architecture Insights:**\n- **Per-stream CPU-GPU work**: Each OS thread = one per-thread CUDA stream (implicit via `cudaStreamPerThread`)\n- **Pre-allocated pools**: Eliminates per-AIR mutex contention by reusing buffers across work items in same thread\n- **Round-robin distribution**: Balances fixed overhead + height-proportional costs without cost model\n- **Current-stream sync barriers**: Ensures async-free visibility and peak memory tracking correctness\n- **ADD-to-output pattern**: Enables bucket-based accumulation across multiple kernel launches on same stream"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb8ef42e-0257-49b2-9589-b530fe7ebc91","uuid":"8b218c61-1045-4522-828b-bfe3a845c81b","timestamp":"2026-04-14T00:15:27.450Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the multi-stream pattern used in this CUDA Rust codebase. Specifically:\n\n1. In `crates/cuda-backend/src/round0.rs` or wherever Round 0 multi-streaming is implemented - find the pattern for `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS`, `std::thread::scope`, per-thread work items, `current_stream_sync()`, and how per-thread resources are pre-allocated\n\n2. Find how `current_stream_sync()` is called/imported and how `cudaStreamPerThread` works in this codebase\n\n3. Find the `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` FFI function signature (in the cuda/ bindings) to understand what parameters it takes\n\n4. Find `_stacked_reduct