{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AM49XgAD3JsYMef1CxY6Gb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me summarize the results:\n\n| Metric | Before APC 300 | After APC 300 | Change |\n|--------|---------------|---------------|--------|\n| STARK excl trace | 1382ms | 1558ms | +176ms (1.13x worse) |\n| LogUp GKR | 531ms | 724ms | +193ms (1.36x worse) |\n| Round 0 | 242ms | 238ms | -4ms |\n| MLE Rounds | 172ms | 170ms | -2ms |\n| Stacked Reduction | 75ms | 74ms | -1ms |\n\n\n| WHIR | 101ms | 101ms | 0ms |\n| Trace Commit | 256ms | 247ms | -9ms |\n\nNow looking at the APC 100 configuration, I'm seeing a similar pattern where the main proving components degrade significantly—STARK and LogUp both get slower by around 13-27%—while the smaller operations remain relatively stable.\n\nComparing across all APC levels, the LogUp regression is consistent at both APC 300 and APC 100, but disappears entirely at APC 0 since that uses sequential fallback. The Stacked Reduction itself barely moves at any setting. This is clearly not working as intended—the improvement I was hoping for at APC 300 is only 1ms, nowhere near the 15ms threshold I need, and I'm actually seeing a 176ms regression in STARK instead.\n\nThe root cause is becoming clear: APC 100 has 360 AIR instances, which puts it well above the 100-instance threshold, so the multi-stream path activates there too and shows the same regression pattern. Only APC 0 with 99 instances stays unaffected because it falls back to sequential execution. The multi-stream buffer allocations are degrading GKR performance in the subsequent segments.\n\nI need to write up the report, create a log entry, commit this, and then revert the changes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2464,"cache_read_input_tokens":140818,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemera